  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA large upper level ridge will amplify into the Midwest and Great \nLakes regions throughout the coming week with temperatures remaining \nvery mild by mid to late November standards. Surface high pressure \nwill prevail with little movement from where it is currently \ncentered over the Appalachian mountains. One more night with \nlows around freezing is expected tonight, with clear skies and \nlight southerly winds promoting at least some localized patchy \nfog for Wednesday morning. Otherwise, temperatures will continue\nto climb each day this week. Southerly flow and increased WAA \nwill funnel in much warmer air from the Gulf and southern US. \nHigh temperatures will soar into the mid to upper 60s by late in\nthe week, which is 15 to 20 degrees above normal for this time \nof the year!\n\nAs the ridge breaks down by late in the week, rain chances return by \nSaturday night into Sunday with the passage of a cold front. Given \nthat this is several days away, the overall pattern evolution may \nchange. There are still uncertainties as to how much moisture this \nfront will have to work with so low to medium PoPs persist with 20-\n40% chances. Following the frontal passage, temperatures will \nmoderate back towards seasonable levels with highs in the 40s. There \nare no real signs of winter yet as the mild weather pattern looks to \nhold at least through the end of meteorological fall.",
